C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Homework 3. Vehicle Shipment Problem in GAMS
       
      THE PROBLEM:
      How many vehicles to go from each supplier to each dealership to minimize the vehicle cost. 
      Suppliers: Kansas City, Dallas
       
      Data are as fol-lows:
       
      Supplier            Shipment cost                                              Resource Availability   
                           Minneapolis    New York      San Francisco     Seattle     Resource Availability
      Kansas City          4              12            18                18          1000
      Dallas               9              15            17                21          800
       
      Demand
      Minneapolis         400
      New York            250
      San Francisco       450
      Seattle             450
       
       
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      Talha Quddoos
      talha.quddoos@usu.edu
      September 28, 2026
  31   
  32  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  33  * Setting up sets for supply nodes and demand nodes
  34  SETS
  35      i suppliers /KansasCity, Dallas/
  36      j dealerships /Minneapolis,NewYork,SanFrancisco,Seattle/;
  37   
  38   
  39   
  40  * 2. * Setting up parameters for available resources at supplier and the demand/sale at the dealerships
  41   
  42  PARAMETERS
  43      supply(i) vehicles in supplier inventory/KansasCity 1000, Dallas 800/
  44      demand(j) vehicle sales or demand at dealerships  /Minneapolis 400,NewYork 250,SanFrancisco 450,Seattle 450 /;
  45   
  46   
  47  * Cost matrix from supply to demand
  48      table costToShip(i,j)
  49                  Minneapolis NewYork SanFrancisco Seattle
  50      KansasCity  4           12      18           18
  51      Dallas      9           15      17           21;
  52   
  53   
  54   
  55  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  56  VARIABLES
  57      x(i,j) vehicles to ship from a factory i to dealership j (Number)
  58      z  shipment cost;
  59  * Non-negativity constraints
  60  POSITIVE VARIABLES x;
  61   
  62  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  63  EQUATIONS
  64      objective equation representing objective function
  65      supplyConstraint(i) equation representing supply constraint
  66      demandConstraint(j) equation representing demand constraint;
  67   
  68  objective.. sum((i,j),costToShip(i,j)*x(i,j)) =e=z;
  69  supplyConstraint(i).. sum(j, x(i,j))=L=supply(i);
  70  demandConstraint(j).. sum(i, x(i,j))=E=demand(j);
  71   
  72  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  73  MODEL Shipment /all/;
  74  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  75  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  76   
  77   
  78  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  79  * Solve the Manufacturing model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  80  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  81  SOLVE Shipment USING LP Minimize z;
  82   
  83   
  84  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


